  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Oak Springs’ name comes from the Navajo term “T’eel ch’ínít’i’í,” meaning “marsh cattail in a row,” reflecting its wetland landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oak_springs_arizona",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community’s roots trace back to the late 1800s when a trading post sprang up beside the Atlantic and Pacific Railroad, linking Navajo herders with settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oak_springs_arizona",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sacred Dziihidzoh Hill, just south of Oak Springs, has long hosted Navajo prayers and once yielded copper and turquoise nuggets used in ceremonies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oak_springs_arizona",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1860s Long Walk, the deep washes around Oak Springs hid Diné people fleeing U.S. soldiers, offering a natural refuge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oak_springs_arizona",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Oak Springs’ chapter is famed for weaving, with artisans like Isabel Deschinny teaching dyeing techniques that use about 300 native plants.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oak_springs_arizona",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 2013, a new 11‑mile road from Houck to Pine Springs was paved, easing travel between Oak Springs and the regional capital.",
